We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_{\phi}(\Omega)\text{ and }D^{s}v\in W^{s,p}(\Omega)^{n}\right\},
  \end{equation*}
  where the function $f$ is strictly convex and satisfies a $p$-growth condition with respect to the gradient variable. We have also established higher differentiability results for solutions within a domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^n$. This is achieved by comparing our problem to a trace obstacle problem and by a careful analysis of the regularity of the associated free boundary. The findings are particularly relevant for problems arising from a local nonlocal operator inside the domain and when the solution is constrained by a superharmonic function. We have, therefore, established that if the function $f$ is of $(2-s)f$-regularity with respect to the gradient variable, the solutions will possess a gradient of quadratic growth, and as a result, the solutions will be of $f$-regularity, and thus possess higher differentiability properties within the domain $\Omega$.
